# Hydronium developer-experience inventory

This is an implementation inventory, not a promise list. “Verified” means a
source, artifact, or browser gate exists in this repository as of 2026-09-24.
“Needs work” is intentionally concrete: it names the gap a developer would
feel, rather than treating an unfinished checklist as a defect in itself.

## The product shape

Hydronium is a Lua/LuaJIT reactive UI system with one component and signal
model across DOM, server rendering, terminal Ink, and Lab hosts. LUAX is
optional authoring syntax; normal Lua is always a first-class runtime path.

The strongest design property is the separation of pure policy from a host:

```text
application state / components
        │
hydronium/core ── router / query / table / virtual
        │
DOM + SSR + Meteorite       Ink terminal       Lab adapters
```

That lets a route, form, table model, or virtual-list geometry stay testable
without a browser or terminal. It is the right substrate for a pleasant Lua
DX: direct APIs in normal Lua, with higher-level hosts as adapters rather than
parallel frameworks.

## Golden-path acceptance criterion

From an empty directory, a Lua developer should be able to create a Hydronium
application, open it in a supported editor with working diagnostics and
completion, run it, modify a component with HMR, execute its tests, produce a
release artifact, and reproduce the build from its lockfile **without manually
configuring framework internals**.

This is the acceptance test for near-term work. A feature should either make
that path smoother or close a demonstrated advanced use case. It keeps the
project from confusing legitimate substrate work—masonry, adaptive overscan,
automatic RTL normalization, enormous benchmarks, or additional grouping
semantics—with the work that makes a first-time adopter think “this is nice.”

